WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.070
So my principal interest
is understanding

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:04.180
what goes on inside our heads.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:06.900
And I'm convinced that one of
the defining features of human

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.990
intelligence is that we can
understand stories in a way

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:11.820
that other primates can't.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.800
So we start with fairy
tales, and we end up

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.630
with the cases of law
school and business

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.500
school and medical school, and
all throughout our education,

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.070
we're dealing with stories.

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:22.080
So believing as I do that
stories are extremely

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.180
important, it's
natural, then, to try

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.615
to build systems that
understand stories

00:00:26.615 --> 00:00:28.740
and that shed light on what
the story understanding

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.640
process is all about.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.400
So let me demonstrate
this by showing you

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.740
a little bit of the
Genesis system at work.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:39.060
I will start with just
how Genesis understands

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:42.000
ordinary English by
typing in some very

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.220
simple sentences
about things happening

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.790
in the physical world.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:50.900
So we can say, for
example, so there's

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:54.170
a description of a trajectory,
and the Genesis system has

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:55.880
a variety of experts
that understand

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.580
that sentence in terms of
it being a trajectory that

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:01.890
goes along a path and ends
up in a particular place.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:03.795
We can add a little
complexity by saying

00:01:03.795 --> 00:01:05.420
the bird flew to the
top of a tree, oh,

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:09.740
because a cat appeared.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:12.260
So now we have a
transition and a cause

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:15.050
to complement the movement
along our trajectory.

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:16.460
And of course, we
are not limited

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.020
to things that happen
in the physical world.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:19.580
We can move into
an abstract world.

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:30.930
That's movement just like a cat
moving to the top of a tree,

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:34.257
but this time it's
in a political space

00:01:34.257 --> 00:01:36.090
moving toward an abstract
destination rather

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:37.470
than a physical one.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:40.530
But still, we see the
trajectory apparatus light up,

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:43.080
and now we've added the goal.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:44.670
I can modify that a
little bit and say

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:48.780
the president asked Iraq
to move toward democracy,

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.530
and this time, we light
up the persuasion box.

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:58.375
And if I say forced instead,
that lights up coercion.

00:01:58.375 --> 00:02:00.000
So now, we're going
to move on and have

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.410
a look at a very simply stated
version of Shakespeare's

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:08.220
play, Macbeth.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:11.730
So the system is reading
away, and everything

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:13.230
that you see inside
of a white box

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:16.290
is explicitly told in the
story, but all this stuff

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:19.740
in the gray boxes has been
inferred by common sense rules.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:22.590
For example, there's
one here that says

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:24.070
that Macbeth murders Duncan.

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:26.753
So Duncan naturally
becomes dead,

00:02:26.753 --> 00:02:28.170
but that's not all
that we can do.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:31.020
We can look for more extended
patterns in this thing

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.728
we call the elaboration graph.

00:02:32.728 --> 00:02:34.770
For example, we can look
for the revenge pattern,

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:36.810
and we find it there.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:41.070
Macbeth harms Macduff that means
that Macbeth angers Macduff.

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:44.370
That leads to Macduff killing
Macbeth, so Macduff in the end

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:45.600
harms Macbeth.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.493
So when you put all that
together, it's a revenge.

00:02:48.493 --> 00:02:50.910
Now, we can zoom back out, and
see even a more complicated

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:51.493
pattern there.

00:02:51.493 --> 00:02:53.070
There's the empiric victory.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:55.680
That's because Macbeth does
something that initially leads

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.068
to him being happier,
he becomes king,

00:02:58.068 --> 00:02:59.610
but eventually, that
leads to Macbeth

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.870
being very unhappy
because he gets murdered.

